A||||14||GT Bastard||Who's ported heads/intake?||||5||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||05-12-2000||12:13 AM||||My dad has convinced me to pull my heads so that he can port and polish them for me, and I'm just wondering what kind of gains can be expected.  I was reading some info on porting, and one particular source noted that ported heads can decrease low-end torque.  At the same time, I'm removing manifolds to be ported, and I'm thinking that might help balance it out(am I right?)  Any recommendations?  How bout 1.6 rockers while I'm at it??  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Taijiguy||05-12-2000||12:25 AM||||Porting can give you some good results. One word of advice, don't remove the little "bump" that's inside the cavities on the bottom. The designers put alot of effort into the heads, and put those there to help with air flow. I've read removing them can actually prove counterproductive. (I left mine intact, and just smoothed them out)Also, look inside the exhaust manifolds, and you'll see some serious restriction there. Die grind the excess steel all the way back to the flange, you'll open the exhaust manifolds way up by doing that. Good luck!<P>-John||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierosound||05-12-2000||12:39 AM||||I agree.  You CAN lose low end torque depending on how the heads are ported.  Gasket matching the ports and smoothing the irregularities in the pockets, runners and bowls will give you better flow and more horsepower because you have removed restrictions. <P>What you want to AVOID is enlarging the overall volume of the ports (runners) within the manifold, as this will decrease the velocity of the air flowing through the manifold, thus causing a power loss.<P>Porting the 60 degree V6: <P>Stock Fiero iron heads have a "vane" or sharks fin in the floor of the intake port. Do not remove this fin when cleaning up the port---it provides a 17% increase in airflow. Match the intake ports to the intake manifold gasket and remove casting irregularities from the port walls. When porting the Intake passages, do not enter to port farther than 3/4" or you may cause head, valve or valve guide damage. Blend any sharp edges and machined steps in the valve bowl to form a smooth approach to the valve seat. The stock HO heads have 4-angle valve seats. This design provides optimum airflow. Contour the exhaust valve bowl to form a smooth venturi under the valve seat. The exhaust ports will require less work as they closer to the size to the exhaust header openings. <P>NOTE:<BR>Excessive milling of the head can lead to head gasket failure unless the intake manifold is also machined. For this reason, milling of the head should be kept to the minimum required to true the deck surface. Also note that machining the manifold to match the head may result in failure of the valve cover gaskets. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||05-12-2000||01:41 PM||||Has anyone actually done this?||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||05-12-2000||01:41 PM||||Has anyone actually done this?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||05-12-2000||02:43 PM||||I have.  Well, I didn't personally, but my heads have been ported.  I think it's just a matter of smoothing the rough edges.  I don't think you'll realize any gains by polishing.  Just take you time and remove what you think doesn't belong.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||05-12-2000||07:46 PM||||I don't know how old your dad is are how knowledgeable he is. Some of the old style porting later proved to decrease power in some cases when racers began bench-flowing their heads.<P>Now in most cases, some mild pocket porting is quite helpful in gaining extra flow. This is also sometimes called bowl blending. I've done it on a pair of small bock heads, 441 castings that I first had the shop machine out for bigger valves (2.02"/1.60"). You just want to smooth the rough edges up, not to enlargen anything.<P>Use your Demel and fine grinding stones to round off the top of the valve guide too, but don't grind the guide down flush, it has to be that high to support the valve. Fierosound mentioned the "vane" in the runner. All literature I've seen has said the same, leave it, it helps flow.<P>Polishing was once thought to help flow, but now has been proven it really don't gain much, if any. Some studies have indicated a little roughness to the metal of the intake runners helps flow.||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||05-12-2000||09:34 PM||||How exactly can the intake runners be ported?  It looks like it would be extremely difficult.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||05-12-2000||09:53 PM||||You don't port the runners, other than gasket matching on the manifold sides. Pocket porting just smoothes out the sharp bends in the bowls located right below the valves.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Taijiguy||05-14-2000||11:32 AM||||This is a somewhat old thread, and I don't know if it's still being watched, but just in case:<BR>Yes, I did mine. The best method (for me) was with a pencil adapter on the Dremel, and a variety of stones. (I ate up a few of the tapered ones, they were invaluable getting into some of the tighter places)I did go in and smooth out the lower and intermediate plenums also, but there's not much you can do with the upper plenum as it's so flat (We ARE talkin about a six here, right?) <BR>Smoothing out the ports, and opeinging up the exhaust manifolds like I mentioned in my earlier post will give you some results. Opening the exhaust manifold by itself is probably good for a few extra horses, as it'll evacuate so much more efficiently. It is a bunch of work, but can help out.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000010||theogre||05-14-2000||11:58 AM||||If you are using a Dremmel tool use a variable speed one and keep the speed down. It makes the tool easier to control.<P>The prefered abrasives are sand paper tapers and rolls spun in a variable speed die grinder. You have more choices of grits.<P>Don't allow the shafts or chucks to contact anything. They can dig nasty holes really fast.<P>It's been a long time since I did any of this. Allot has been learned by the engine makers/builders sinse then.<P>If you are doing this you'll want a couple sets of gaskets at least. You want one set to work with and another to actually install incase you mangle the first set. You also want to record waht gaskets you used. If you have to pull the thing apart latter you want to use the same gaskets. there can be considerable diferance between brands.<P>Don't do anything to runners but knock off protruding material. You can't hand polish runners without damaging them.<P>Unless you're very good at this Don't alter the head beyond smoothing rough areas. You can majorly screw up performance with bad choices of trimming.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||GT Bastard||05-14-2000||02:26 PM||||If I'm just going to smooth them out, maybe I shouldn't even bother sending the heads to my dad.... Is this a pretty simple task with a Dremel tool?||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||05-14-2000||02:40 PM||||Mainly time consuming. I was very careful to make sure I didn't screw anything up. It took me over half an hour per port on my small block heads. Yep, time consuming. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phaeton||05-15-2000||02:35 AM||||Put two layers of masking tape on the valve seats and machined surfaces.  That tiny split second of warning when the grinder hits the tape can save lots of rework time.<P>I've done several small block cast iron heads and two sets of 3.1 aluminum heads.<P>A carbide tip for stock removal and a stone or flap wheel for finish worked best for me.<P>All the advice in this tread is good.  The factory did a very fine job with the volume and basic shape of the ports and runners.  Our job is to correct for minor core shifts and do the final hand finish under the valves where the assembly line couldn't.<P>Time and patience, conservative is good here. ||||reg||12
Z||000014||mwbackus||05-15-2000||09:52 AM||||Heres some real good info on head porting:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.sa-motorsports.com/diyguide.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sa-motorsports.com/diyguide.htm</A> <P>Their "Deluxe" 260001 kit is available from Summit for only $39.95.<P>Mike B.||||reg||1
